## 13. Conclusion

P0 Guard Beastmode is framed around a central application-security principle: an application should not be trusted solely because of the name it presents or the identity it claims.

Program-name blocking supplies a direct policy mechanism for prohibited applications. Identity-claim comparison provides a separate means of detecting discrepancies between observed claims and trusted reference information. Memory Crystal is the named reference component associated with this comparison in the design described by this paper.

The strength of the resulting security control depends on how identity evidence is collected, how reference information is protected, where policy is enforced, and whether enforcement outcomes are independently verifiable.

This whitepaper establishes the scope and evaluation criteria for Beastmode. Implementation-specific claims should be finalized against the relevant source code, runtime behavior, and test evidence before this draft is designated a verified implementation specification.

